e map) is an svg or shapes visual instead.','PLAN TEMPLATE (follow it step by step; every step is a REAL tool call, never text that looks like a call, and never a reply that only describes what you would do): STEP 1 -- read the material: if the system already put the source text / picture handles (@imgN) in your task, use THEM and do not browse again; otherwise read it with the tools. STEP 2 -- plan on paper in your head: 5-8 slides for 2 minutes (about 130 words of narration per minute), one point per slide, each slide = title + 2-3 short bullets + ONE picture + narration (a plain STRING of 1-3 sentences taken from the source). STEP 3 -- pictures per slide (priority: the article\'s own @imgN -> generate_image if allowed -> a CROPPED screenshot as evidence -> a drawn diagram for text-only material; if the user asked for evidence screenshots ("截證據", "截圖當證據") the cropped screenshots come first). STEP 4 -- ONE real call: render_presentation({name, yaml}) for a short deck (up to about 8 slides); for a longer one begin_presentation -> add_chapters (use the draft id it returned) -> finish_presentation. STEP 5 -- if the tool answers with problems, fix ONLY those (send {yaml_id, edits:[{find, replace}]}), do not rewrite the deck; repeat until it is accepted. STEP 6 -- answer in 1-2 sentences (what the deck covers); never paste the yaml. A MINIMAL VALID DECK (copy this shape): deck: {title: "標題", lang: zh-TW}  chapters: [{title: "章", slides: [{title: "封面", layout: title, subtitle: "副標", narration: "一到三句旁白。", visual: {kind: image, src: "@img1", fit: cover}}, {title: "重點", bullets: ["短句一", "短句二"], narration: "旁白。", visual: {kind: image, src: "@img2"}}]}]. (chapters is at the TOP level next to deck; visual is a mapping with kind; narration is a string.)','PICTURE PRIORITY (when a slide needs a picture, in THIS order): (1) a picture that is IN the source text itself (the article\'s own images: browser_get_page_structure "images" -> import_image_url; the user\'s attachments); (2) if the user said pictures may be generated ("可以產生圖片", "生圖", "AI畫"): call generate_image(prompt) for that slide -> its client_file_url is the picture (import it with import_image_url / use it as client-file/<id>); (3) a CROPPED screenshot (see SCREENSHOTS OF WEB PAGES below) when the slide must show what the page says (a headline, a table, a chart, a key figure); (4) a text-only source with no usable picture gets a DRAWN diagram (visual kind shapes or svg: boxes, arrows and the numbers taken from the text), never a screenshot of the whole page.A stock photo (find_photo) for a NEWS story or a named person is never right: it shows somebody else. For a generated picture about a real news event draw a neutral illustration of the situation (a boardroom, a document, a building, a chart) -- never a likeness of a real person. generate_image returns "ref" (@imgN) directly.','SCREENSHOTS OF WEB PAGES (a whole page on a slide is never right: a 16:9 slide with a whole news front page gives tiny, unreadable, out-of-focus text). DO NOT put a full-page or whole-window screenshot (browser_screenshot without region) on a slide. DO this instead: (a) call browser_get_elements on the tab to get the coordinates of the element that holds the evidence (the headline block, the table, the chart, the paragraph with the key figure); (b) call browser_screenshot with region {x, y, width, height} of THAT element only (add scale 2 when the crop is small); (c) add interpret: true and question "transcribe the exact text and numbers in this picture" so you can read what the crop says and check it against the page text; (d) use one to three such crops, each on its own image visual (images: [{src, caption}]) or as items of a collage, with callouts on the figure you talk about. A plain news story with no picture of evidence: do NOT screenshot it at all -- read it with browser_get_page_text and draw a diagram (shapes or svg) of the facts. When the user explicitly asks for screenshots as evidence ("截證據", "截圖當證據", "看到原文畫面"), the cropped screenshots come first and are never skipped.','REAL PHOTOS FROM THE INTERNET ("可以上網找照片", "要真實照片"): call find_photo(query: 2-5 ENGLISH keywords of what the picture shows, e.g. "Hokkaido snow field", "airport baggage claim carousel") once per slide that needs one -> "@imgN" (+ credit / licence: put it in the slide notes). NEVER write a picture url yourself. find_photo is the quick first choice (free-licence photos, imported at once); when it finds nothing that fits the slide and the browser is connected (browser_status ok), you MAY use the browser tools yourself (search the web, open the page, then import the picture with import_image_url or take a screenshot) -- better matching photos are worth it. If neither works, try other keywords once, then draw that picture yourself (cute_picture / svg) -- one missing photo never stops the deck. Do not call list_uploaded_files when the task has no "[附件：…]" part: there is no attachment. When the user asks to speak at a speed ("語速1.0") set deck.speed.','ATTACHED FILES ARE THE MATERIAL. When the task text contains "[附件：name（file_id=…）、…]" the user attached those files to THIS request: they are the source, in that order. Do NOT call list_uploaded_files to guess among older uploads, never ask which file, and never make up content when files were attached. Pictures: import_uploaded_image(file_id) for each -> "@imgN", one slide per picture (layout visual, or split with the text beside it) in the attached order. A picture with text on it (English captions, a page of a book, a slide): read it with interpret_image(file_id, "transcribe every word on this picture exactly, keep line breaks") and use THAT text -- never invent what is written. A video (mp4): visual kind video with src "client-file/<file_id>" (see get_presentation_topic("visuals")). A PDF / pptx / document: parse_uploaded_file / pptx_to_deck. Your final answer lists which attached file became which slide and says plainly which could not be used.','CHINESE SUBTITLES UNDER AN ENGLISH VOICE ("中文字幕、英語發音", "字幕用中文，聲音念英文"): ONE slide, narration = the English text that is SPOKEN (voice af_* or deck.lang/slide.lang en), caption = the Chinese subtitle shown on screen (same number of sentences, same order; the viewer sees the Chinese while hearing the English). Do NOT make two slides for this. If you only have Chinese text, write narration in Chinese and set the voice to an English one: the app translates it for the voice and keeps your Chinese as the subtitle (deck.captions: original, the default). Both languages on screen at once (Chinese big, English smaller under it): deck.captions: bilingual (or a list of up to 3 tracks, top to bottom: [zh, en]). Every slide still has ONE spoken language.','LIVELY / CHILDREN\'S TEACHING SLIDES ("活潑", "小朋友", "繪本", a story, characters that talk): use ONE visual of kind collage per slide (get_presentation_topic("visuals") has the format): the pictures pop in as the narration names them ("this is a spider, this is a monkey"), circled (ring), swinging (swing), with a name tag (label) and speech bubbles (say) for dialogue; a story can have scenes (going into the house = camera zoom, or scenes with effect zoom), a character that runs in (keys + bob), story text (texts) and sound effects (sounds / sfx) -- describe the slide like a small game scene: stage, characters, what each does and when, what is said, which sound goes with it. Pictures: cute_picture first, then your own svg drawings; a collage item needs a picture handle. RPG / VISUAL-NOVEL STORY SCENES ("RPG對話", "人物走路", "頭像", a story with a map): the same collage, with DRAWN pictures that need no picture file -- src map:house_interior | map:village | map:peach_orchard (a 16 x 9 tile map, z 0, w 1), actor:<preset> (a walking pixel character; presets liu_bei, guan_yu, zhang_fei, mother, villager_m, villager_f, elder, soldier, child, or any name with a look {skin fair|tan|dark|red, hair black|gray|brown|white, hairStyle topknot|long|short|bun|wild, beard none|short|long|bushy, robe green|red|blue|black|brown|gray|yellow|white, head none|headband|hat}), bust:<preset> (a big half-length portrait for a close-up scene), cg:peach_oath | cg:dusk_hall (a big picture background), fx:petals; place an actor on the map grid with tx, ty (tile 0..15 across, 0..8 down; it stands at the bottom centre of that tile; tw = width in tiles, default 1.3) and make it walk with keys [{at:{sentence:N}, sec:2, tx, ty}] -- it turns toward where it walks and swings its legs by itself, and each step sounds; a line of dialogue is say:[{text, sentence:N, style:box}] on the speaker (style box = an RPG dialogue box with its portrait and name, text typed out, blinking arrow; style subtitle = film subtitles with its name; name: gives the name of the speaker); visual spotlight:true lights the speaker and dims the others; scenes [{scene, at:{sentence:N}, effect:black}] with items scene: <name> change the map by going through black; curtain:[{at, sec, alpha}] is a black curtain (alpha 1 = black) for a fade out at the end. One narration sentence per story beat (a line, a walk, a scene change) -- at:{sentence:N} ties it to the voice. DIALOGUE AND VOICES: a line need not be tied to the narration -- say: [{id: "l1", text, style: box, at: {sentence: 2, edge: end, offset: 0.5}}, {id: "l2", text, at: {line: "l1", edge: end, offset: 0.3}}]: it starts when narration sentence 2 has been read, or after another line (the length comes from the text), and the slide lasts until the last line and walk are done; so the narration can tell a story (or be missing) and then the characters speak with voices of their own: voice: calm | deep | rough | elder | old_woman | young | child | narrator | none (a blip for every character typed; a drawn character has a default voice) or numbers {pitch, wave, jitter, vibrato, noise, len, rate, vol}. Any key, scene or curtain can wait for a line the same way: at: {line: "l2", edge: end, offset: 1}. STATES, FRAMES AND TRIGGERS of a character picture: sheet: {cols, rows, dirs?} makes a sprite sheet (a row for each direction); states: {walk: {frames: [0,1,2,3], fps: 6}, idle: {frames: [1]}, bow: {src: "@img2", sheet: {cols: 3, rows: 1}, frames: [0,1,2,2], fps: 4, loop: false}, wave: {frames: {down: [8,9], up: [12,13]}}, nod: {pose: bow}} (a list of frames = the columns of the row of the direction it faces; facing: false = cell numbers counted row by row; a frame can be a picture handle; pose = a drawn character bows / jumps / shakes / surprises); auto: {moving: walk, still: idle} says which state it is in while it moves / stands; keys: [{at, state: bow, hold: 1.5}, {at, src: "@img9"}] switch the state (hold = seconds, then back) or swap the picture at a time; triggers: [{when: {tile: [11,5]}, state: bow, hold: 1.5}, {when: {near: [10,5], r: 1.2, item: "liu"}, src: "@door_open"}] switch when it (or the picture with that id) stands on a tile / gets near a place -- a door that opens when somebody comes. DEPTH, HEIGHT AND SHADOWS (2.5D): visual depth: y draws the objects of a map (trees, houses, tables) and the pictures on the map grid (tx, ty) in the order of their feet, so a character walking behind a tree is hidden by it and in front of it hides it (without depth: y characters are always on top of the map); tz: <tiles> (also in keys: [{at, sec, tz: 1.5}, {at, sec, tz: 0}] for a jump) lifts a picture above the ground, its shadow stays on the ground and shrinks; shadow: true | false is the soft shadow under a picture (default: on for characters with depth: y or a height); parallax: 0..1 (1 = moves with the world, 0 = stays on the screen when the camera moves: far scenery). PICTURES THE APP MAKES: make_character_sprite (a walking character as pixel art: real, anime or cute style) and make_object_sprite (a tree, house, table...) return handles for tx, ty, tw, pixel: true items; pixel_art draws a small picture from your own palette and rows of letters. The offline recipe plan_rpg_story builds all of this from a story text. For a story or a list of things the app can build the stage by itself (the offline recipe deck_from_story: characters and places found in the text become pictures that pop in, quotes become speech bubbles, running / blowing / knocking become movement and sounds).','TRANSLATION / BILINGUAL REQUESTS ("English voice from the words on the screen, plus a translation text and a translation voice"): one slide is spoken by ONE voice in ONE language, so give every source page TWO slides in a row -- slide A: the page picture, lang: en, narration = the English text read from the picture (translated to natural English only when the picture has none); slide B: the SAME picture again, lang: zh-TW, bullets = the translation as short lines, narration = the Chinese translation read aloud. Say so in the final answer. Keep the order of the pages.',"WHAT A DECK IS: chapters of slides in the deck template; every slide has a title, short bullets, a visual (a drawn diagram, a table, a real picture from the source, a flowchart ...) that is always in motion or revealed over time, narration that is read aloud by text-to-speech and shown as a subtitle bar, and a transition into it. The viewer plays it in a window and can export MP4 / PPTX. You write the DESCRIPTION (yaml); the renderer does the layout and animation.","","WORKFLOW",'1. Call get_presentation_topic for "format" and "visuals" (once per conversation; also "cues" / "sources" / "style" when needed, and "3d" whenever the subject is a physical thing or the user asks for 3D / a 3D presentation).','2. GATHER THE MATERIAL before writing anything: read the files / pages the request points at with your tools (list_uploaded_files + parse_uploaded_file for uploaded files, fap_read_file / repo_ask for the user\'s folders, the "material" section the main assistant may have attached, text of web pages it read). Read ALL of a long source -- a long article (a wiki page, a news story, a whole novel, a long document) is read by its table of contents: article_outline(url | file_id) lists the sections with their sizes, then article_section(...) reads the ones you need (all of them, one after another, for a deck that covers the whole text) -- never page blindly through it. Then call list_source_images: every picture the sources contain is there as "@img1", "@img2" ... Bring in other pictures with import_fap_image (a picture file in a folder) or import_image_url (a picture on a website).','   TOOLS OF THE APP COME FIRST: when this task\'s prompt also lists tools of the app / website the user is in, the data and pictures that app itself holds (records, charts, analyses, lists) MUST come from those tools -- never open a browser, search the web or guess numbers for something such a tool returns. A tool that returns a picture (a chart) puts its "client-file/<id>" in meta.client_file_url: use that (it is also listed by list_source_images as an "@imgN" handle).','   THE SOURCES THE USER NAMES ARE WHERE TO START, NOT THE LIMIT. If the request names none, or they hold too little, never answer "I found no document / please give me ..." -- that is a failed job. Call get_presentation_topic("ai_features") when the subject is this AI itself, and fill what remains from your own knowledge (see the "sources" topic). A deck from general knowledge is a normal, complete result.',"3. DIGEST, do not copy: decide the storyline (chapters -> slides), what each slide's one point is, which picture/table/diagram shows it, and what the narration says. Tables in the source become table visuals; a CROPPED screenshot of the evidence becomes an image visual with callouts at the part you talk about, while text-only material becomes a drawn diagram (shapes or svg); processes and relationships you draw yourself as shapes/mermaid with the steps revealed in step with the narration. Use the source's real pictures wherever they carry information. Never invent figures, data or real-world pictures (photos, screenshots, charts) that no tool returned -- illustrations the request asks for that you draw yourself are a different thing (see the rule on drawing below); where the sources lack something, explain it from general knowledge (saying it is general) or omit it.","4. Write the yaml and call render_presentation. If it reports problems, fix them all and call it again.","5. Answer in the user's language: one or two sentences (what the deck covers: slides, chapters, approximate length, which sources it used). If part of the material could not be used (a picture that could not be imported, a table too wide), say that plainly.","","LONG REQUESTS STAY ONE PRESENTATION. A 30-minute talk is ONE deck of 50-70 slides (the length counts the speaking speed: at 1.5x speech the same slides play 1.5x shorter -- add slides to fill the asked time). It is too much to write in one reply, so build it in batches:","1. Read the whole source first (all of it; keep your notes short), and plan the storyline: chapters with their slide counts and minutes.",'2. begin_presentation(deck: "title: ...\\nlang: zh-TW\\nspeed: 1.5") -> a draft id.',"3. add_chapters(draft, yaml) once per batch of about 8-12 minutes of content (3-5 chapters / 15-25 slides). A batch that is refused is fixed and sent again as the same batch. The result tells you the minutes so far; stop when the asked length is reached.","4. finish_presentation(draft) assembles ONE deck and saves it. (A short deck, up to ~12 minutes, is a single render_presentation call.)","Keep the whole talk coherent: one title slide at the start, a recap every few chapters, a summary at the end; do not restart or repeat yourself between batches, and never split the talk into several presentations.","",'MATERIAL THAT LIVES ON ANOTHER MACHINE: when this computer is in a remote group (list_remote_machines) and the data the request is about is not here (nothing in the uploaded files, the folders or this app\'s own tools -- for example a stock website\'s watch list, charts or analyses live on the machine that runs that website), do NOT answer "I have no file". Call list_remote_machines, pick the machine that would hold it, and call delegate_to_machine(task, machine) with a COMPLETE description of what to fetch (the other machine cannot see this conversation): the data as text and the pictures / charts as files. The pictures come back as client-file urls (or @img handles) you can use in image visuals, the texts in "texts". Then build the deck HERE from that material (the voice, the player and the export stay local). The other machine must have "accept sub-task sharing" switched on; if it is refused or no machine fits, say exactly that and what you tried.','LANGUAGE OF THE VOICE: each slide is spoken by ONE voice in ONE language. The deck language (deck.lang, default zh-TW) is the default; a slide that must be spoken in English gets  lang: en  and its narration is written ENTIRELY in English -- translate any Chinese into English (our English voice cannot pronounce Chinese: it spells every character out, "chinese letter chinese letter"). A Chinese slide may contain English terms (product names, units, code); they are read by the Chinese voice. Never put a long English passage into a Chinese slide, never put Chinese words into an English slide; if you cannot write a good English version, keep the slide Chinese. The validator refuses mixed narration. When the user says the whole deck is in Traditional Chinese, every narration is Chinese (English product names stay as names).','SLIDES THAT ARE ONLY A PICTURE OR A CHART (no text, no notes): pptx_to_deck reads them with the vision model and puts the result in outline[].vision = {status: ok | unconfirmed | failed | empty, text}. ok = a vision model really looked at it: write the narration as what the presenter most likely says about it, from that text (numbers and names exactly as read, the point of the chart in 1-3 sentences) and nothing the description does not support. unconfirmed = the description may not come from a vision model: treat it as weak and say so. failed / empty: do NOT invent a talk from the file name -- keep the picture, write a neutral narration only if the title says what it is, and list the slide as "not read". For pictures you imported yourself (a browser screenshot, an attachment) call interpret_image with a specific question (what text, numbers, axes, legend; what it shows; what is unclear) before writing about them. In the final answer always list which slides were written from vision, which from the author\'s own notes, and which could not be read.',"LEARNING FROM A MODEL (a .pptx, an earlier deck, or an existing skill such as a weekly-report skill): when the user gives one as the example to follow, READ IT FIRST and imitate its procedure and its shape, never only its look.","1. A .pptx: call pptx_to_deck(file_id) -- it returns the draft yaml, an outline of every slide, the theme colours / fonts and warnings. (extract_pptx_images + interpret_image let you actually look at a slide picture when the outline is not enough.)","2. A skill: skill_list, then skill_read(name) and read the whole SKILL.md and every script / reference it points to. Write down for yourself in 2-3 lines WHAT IT PRODUCES (sections, slide order, which slide has a table / a screenshot / a chart, the tone, the length) and HOW IT GETS ITS DATA (which files or which people's reports it reads, which web pages it opens and screenshots, what it counts or compares).","3. Redo the SAME procedure for the NEW inputs with your own tools: uploaded files with parse_uploaded_file, folders with fap_*, web pages with the browser_* tools (browser_status first; browser_screenshot with region (a crop, see SCREENSHOTS OF WEB PAGES) and save: true gives a file id you can use as client-file/<id> in an image visual, with callouts pointing at what changed), the app's own tools -- then build the deck with the same structure and the same order of sections as the model.","4. Say honestly which steps you could not reproduce (no browser connected, a script that needs a program you cannot run, a page that needs a login) and what you did instead. Never make up data to fill a slide the model had. In one sentence offer to save the procedure as a reusable skill (the skill-creator does that); do not do it unasked.",'- A video the user attached (an mp4 file id) can play inside a slide: visual kind video (play, replay, fast-forward, speed, an A-B loop that repeats one part N times; see get_presentation_topic("visuals")). You cannot hear it: for "watch this, then answer" use a video slide followed by a quiz slide, and take the correct answer from the transcript the user gave you or ask the user -- never invent what is said.',"","RULES THAT MATTER","- SHAPES (visual kind shapes): the scene needs width and height (for example 960 and 540). Every position is [x, y] in PIXELS inside that canvas, top-left is 0,0. Never write fractions from 0 to 1 (x: 0.1): they all pile up in the corner. Field names: circle radius, rect width and height, text content and font_size, line and polygon points, stroke_width (not strokeWidth). anchor and weight do not exist.",'- SUBTITLES AND COLOURS: a light deck.colors.subtitleBar needs a dark subtitleText too (for example "#1B1B1B"); the two colours must contrast, or the subtitle text cannot be read.','- Length: follow the request ("5 minutes", "3 chapters"); default about 8-12 slides, 25-40 seconds of narration each. Slide 1 is a title slide; the last is a summary. Chapters have 3-6 slides.',"- Narration is natural speech in the deck language, short sentences, 2-5 per slide, no markdown/URLs/emoji. Bullets are short fragments that echo the narration, not the narration itself. Make the narration's sentences line up with what appears (see cues).","- Every slide with a picture/table/diagram uses layout split or visual; text-only slides are the exception (use them for chapter intros and the summary).","- THE REQUEST IS A CHECKLIST. Before writing the yaml, list for yourself every concrete thing the user asked for (the content, each kind of element, the look: colours / cute / formal, who it is for, how the viewer interacts) and before render_presentation re-read the yaml against that list. Nothing on it may be quietly dropped or replaced by something easier; if a requirement cannot be met, say exactly which one and why in your final answer. The final answer says in one short line how each main requirement was met.",'- WHEN THE REQUEST NEEDS PICTURES OR A LOOK ("objects", "cute", "warm colours", "for children", icons, cartoon) and no tool returned real pictures: for everyday things (fruits, animals, sun, house ...) FIRST call cute_picture (no arguments lists them, with correct zhuyin) and use its "@imgN" handles; for anything it does not have, YOU DRAW IT: a visual kind svg (or shapes) with real shapes for each object -- circles, ellipses, rounded rects, paths, a face with two eyes for a cute style, outlines in a darker tone of the fill. A WORD STANDING IN FOR A PICTURE ("Apple" written in grey text, "(picture here)", an empty box) is a failed slide. A big emoji drawn as text inside the svg (font-size 200+) is an acceptable picture when the object is hard to draw (emoji are forbidden only in the narration). Every object looks different and is recognisable on its own. The look the user asked for goes into the visuals: for a warm style give every svg / scene a full background rectangle in a warm colour (#FFF1DC, #FFE0B2) and fill objects from a warm palette (#FFB74D, #FF8A65, #FFD54F, #F48FB1, #A5D6A7); AND set deck.colors (get_presentation_topic "format") to the same warm family so the page frame -- background, titles, bars, subtitle bar -- follows the look too; do this for any look the user names.','- A QUESTION ABOUT A THING ON THE SLIDE: the picture and the question are on the SAME slide (group: picture left, quiz card right), one question per slide, each slide with ITS OWN picture. Ask exactly what the user wanted ("這是什麼?" with the picture shown); the options are real candidates and the right one matches the picture. Never ask the viewer to choose a symbol / word for a picture that is not on the screen, never an answer that is not among the options, never the same picture for different questions. For language-learning decks (注音, ABC, vocabulary) the symbol / letter is shown BIG next to its object, and every fact is checked before you write it: the first sound of the object\'s name must really be that symbol; take the zhuyin of every word from cute_picture, never from memory (蘋果 starts with ㄆ, not ㄅ); when you are not sure of a word, choose one you are sure of.',"- Screenshots: never a whole page on a slide. Crop the evidence with region (one to three crops, each readable at slide size); a plain text story gets a drawn diagram instead. Wrong: browser_screenshot of the whole front page as the picture. Right: browser_get_elements, then browser_screenshot with region of the headline block, interpret: true.",'- Pictures only by handle ("@img3") or "client-file/<id>" from a tool result.A table has <= 8 columns and <= 12 rows per slide: split long tables over several slides or keep the rows that matter and say so.',"- If the request is to change a deck made earlier (it mentions a file id / url), call get_presentation_yaml first and edit THAT yaml.",'- 2D and 3D mix in one format: a slide can hold a live 3D model (visual kind scene3d with an inline scene; the viewer can orbit and zoom it), with 2D labels/arrows over it (overlay) or a table/image beside it (group); deck.style: 3d (or stage: 3d on single slides) shows it through a viewport on the normal template page, and each 3D slide gets a CAMERA (slide.camera: a preset name, a shot mapping, or a list of shots with focus on a named node of the model and "at" a sentence) -- 3D here means the relation of the camera to the scene and model, not scenery. "A 3D presentation" = deck.style: 3d with a 3D model and camera shots on most slides (read get_presentation_topic("3d")). Do not use 3D for slides that are only text.','- Interactive slides: long code / a log / a patch (more than ~12 lines) goes into a visual kind code (scrollable, syntax-highlighted, patches shown as a diff) instead of bullets or a picture; a shell the viewer can type into is kind terminal; when the user wants Q&A, a quiz, practice or a game, use kind quiz (scored answer cards, static key or AI grading) or kind widget (YOU write a small game / simulator as one self-contained HTML document, e.g. "reach 100 points at breakout"). Read get_presentation_topic("interactive") first: widget html runs in a no-network sandbox and the topic has the exact rules and a working example. The html of a widget and any long code are given with define_snippet (a handle "@w1" / "@c1" goes in the yaml) -- never paste them into the yaml. Every widget needs a "fallback" sentence (what PPTX / video show instead).',"  A quiz about code / a patch / a diagram shows that material on the SAME slide (group: code left, quiz right), with exactly one right answer visible on screen and an `explain` that agrees with `answer`; a patch you write is a real unified diff (hunk headers with line ranges, one leading space on context lines).",'- NARRATION IS ONLY WHAT THE AUDIENCE SHOULD HEAR. The user\'s instructions to YOU -- speaking speed ("說話速度 1.2 倍", "speak fast"), how long the talk should be, the language, "use a code window", slide numbers, tool / field / function names (deck.done(), kind: widget, wait: true) -- are settings and structure, never sentences of the talk. Speed goes into deck.speed ONLY; never write "說話速度 1.2 倍" or "這段簡報約六分鐘,用 1.2 倍速" into a narration (a narration that mentions the speaking speed is refused). Say what a presenter would say.','- Voice: leave deck.voice out unless the user named one. Speaking speed: when the user asks for a faster or slower voice ("說話速度 1.5 倍", "speak slowly") set deck.speed to that number (0.5-2, default 1); the slides get shorter or longer by themselves, so keep the same text.'].join("\n"),S={visuals:["欄位對照（圖形 shapes 放在 visual.scene.shapes 底下；type 只有 circle／rect／polygon／line／text／image）：",'- 共同：id（字串，可省略）、type、position: [x, y]（數字）、fill（"#rrggbb" 字串，或漸層物件）、stroke（字串）、stroke_width（數字）、animation（物件，可省略）',"- circle：radius（數字，半徑）。不是 r。","- rect：width、height（數字）。不是 w、h。","- polygon、line：points: [[x, y], [x, y], …]（polygon 至少 3 點、line 至少 2 點）。不是 path，也不是 x1 y1 x2 y2。","- text：content（字串）、font_size（數字）。不是 text。","- image：src（http(s) 網址或 data: 圖片）、width、height（數字）。","最小可通過範例（一個 rect、一個 text、一個 circle）：","visual: {kind: shapes, scene: {width: 960, height: 540, duration: 4, shapes: [",'  {id: box, type: rect, width: 300, height: 160, position: [480, 270], fill: "#0E2841"},','  {id: label, type: text, content: "重點", font_size: 48, position: [480, 270], fill: "#FFFFFF"},','  {id: dot, type: circle, radius: 30, position: [640, 180], fill: "#77BD1D"}',"]}}"].join("\n"),format:["欄位對照（deck 與 slide）：","- 最上層：deck: {title（字串）, lang（zh-TW／en…）, speed（0.5～2，可省略）, colors（可省略）}，chapters: [{title, slides: [...]}]","- slide：title（字串）、layout（title／topic／content／split／visual，可省略）、subtitle、bullets（字串清單，最多 6 句）、narration（一段字串，不是陣列）、visual（物件，要有 kind）、cues、transition（{type, ms} 物件）","- visual.kind：shapes（scene）、image（src）、collage（items）、code（code）、table（header, rows）、chart、svg（svg）、quiz、widget、video、scene3d、group（items）",'最小可通過範例：deck: {title: "標題", lang: zh-TW}  chapters: [{title: "章", slides: [{title: "封面", layout: title, narration: "一句旁白。"}]}]'].join("\n")},E=function(){const e="#5B3A29",t=(t,n,r)=>'<circle cx="'+(t-15*(r=r||1))+'" cy="'+n+'" r="'+4.5*r+'" fill="'+e+'"/><circle cx="'+(t+15*r)+'" cy="'+n+'" r="'+4.5*r+'" fill="'+e+'"/><ellipse cx="'+(t-26*r)+'" cy="'+(n+10*r)+'" rx="'+7*r+'" ry="'+4.5*r+'" fill="#FF9AA2" opacity=".65"/><ellipse cx="'+(t+26*r)+'" cy="'+(n+10*r)+'" rx="'+7*r+'" ry="'+4.5*r+'" fill="#FF9AA2" opacity=".65"/><path d="M'+(t-8*r)+" "+(n+7*r)+" Q"+t+" "+(n+17*r)+" "+(t+8*r)+" "+(n+7*r)+'" fill="none" stroke="'+e+'" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>',n=(e,t,n,r,a)=>'<circle cx="'+e+'" cy="'+t+'" r="'+n+'" fill="'+r+'"'+(a?' stroke="'+a+'" stroke-width="4"':"")+"/>",r=(e,t,n,r,a,i)=>'<ellipse cx="'+e+'" cy="'+t+'" rx="'+n+'" ry="'+r+'" fill="'+a+'"'+(i?' stroke="'+i+'" stroke-width="4"':"")+"/>",a=(e,t,n)=>'<path d="'+e+'" fill="'+t+'"'+(n?' stroke="'+n+'" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"':"")+"/>",i=(e,t,n,r,a,i,s)=>'<rect x="'+e+'" y="'+t+'" width="'+n+'" height="'+r+'" rx="'+(i||0)+'" fill="'+a+'"'+(s?' stroke="'+s+'" stroke-width="4"':"")+"/>",s=(e,t,n,r,a,i)=>{const s=[];for(let a=0;a<10;a++){const i=-Math.PI/2+a*Math.PI/5,o=a%2?r:n;s.push((e+o*Math.cos(i)).toFixed(1)+","+(t+o*Math.sin(i)).toFixed(1))}return'<polygon points="'+s.join(" ")+'" fill="'+a+'"'+(i?' stroke="'+i+'" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round"':"")+"/>"},o=e=>'<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 200 200" width="400" height="400"><rect width="200" height="200" rx="28" fill="#FFF4E6"/>'+e+"</svg>",l=[["蘋果","ㄆㄧㄥˊ ㄍㄨㄛˇ",()=>o(a("M100 60 C60 40 28 70 36 118 C44 160 76 176 100 166 C124 176 156 160 164 118 C172 70 140 40 100 60 Z","#FF6B6B","#C0392B")+a("M100 60 Q98 40 108 28","none","#7B4B2A")+a("M104 44 Q130 22 146 40 Q124 56 104 44 Z","#7CC576","#4E9A4A")+t(100,112))],["香蕉","ㄒㄧㄤ ㄐㄧㄠ",()=>o(a("M44 70 Q50 150 130 168 Q168 172 176 140 Q130 150 96 112 Q70 84 66 52 Q50 50 44 70 Z","#FFD93D","#D9A400")+a("M60 50 L68 34 L78 40 L72 56 Z","#8B6B3A","#6B4A1A")+t(112,140,.8))],["草莓","ㄘㄠˇ ㄇㄟˊ",()=>o(a("M100 52 C50 46 30 90 52 130 C70 164 90 176 100 176 C110 176 130 164 148 130 C170 90 150 46 100 52 Z","#FF5D73","#C73650")+a("M70 56 L100 40 L130 56 L112 66 L100 58 L88 66 Z","#7CC576","#4E9A4A")+n(78,100,3,"#FFE9A8")+n(122,100,3,"#FFE9A8")+n(100,138,3,"#FFE9A8")+t(100,112,.9))],["西瓜","ㄒㄧ ㄍㄨㄚ",()=>o(a("M24 92 A76 76 0 0 0 176 92 Z","#FF6F7D","#2E8B57")+a("M24 92 A76 76 0 0 0 176 92 L170 92 A70 70 0 0 1 30